Synthesis
---------

### Central insight

Adding a single, generic "Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis" section to the Narrative user prompt coincided with reproducible editorial drift-company identity and audience scope were subordinated, orchestration was elevated to central positioning, and unsupported audience/validation claims appeared-which corresponded with lower mean quality scores and led to the candidate prompt being rejected and rolled back.

### Evidence

1. Prompt change (controlled): the candidate condition modified only src/Nueyon.Compose.Application/Prompts/Narrative/Narrative.user.prompt.txt to add one new section titled "Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis"; all other prompts and inputs were unchanged (Research Facts 10, 16-19, 52-54). 
2. Quantitative decline in quality: Narrative mean score dropped from 6.00/10 (baseline) to 4.00/10 (candidate); Compose mean score dropped from 5.67/10 to 3.33/10 (Research Facts 25-28). 
3. Consistent qualitative regressions: candidate Narrative outputs ''consistently subordinated company identity and audience scope,'' ''made orchestration the central positioning,'' and ''introduced unsupported validation or audience framing''; all three candidate Narrative runs (and all three candidate Compose runs) failed the gate requiring orchestration to be a supporting explanation rather than the central positioning (Research Facts 29, 35-36). 
4. Concrete candidate artifacts illustrating the drift: candidate Compose outputs included meta-text, implied launch/validation claims, reframed content as an orchestration problem, and named founders/product leaders as audience examples (Research Facts 37-40). 
5. Decision and rollback: evaluators declined to promote the candidate prompt; production Narrative user prompt was restored byte-for-byte to the baseline SHA-256 value (candidate not promoted; rollback verification matching the frozen baseline) (Research Facts 2, 44, 55). 

### What the narrative should emphasize

- Emphasize the two central pieces of evidence that establish the story: (a) the controlled prompt change that added the single generic preservation section (Evidence 1), and (b) the reproducible score drop and gating failures showing consistent editorial drift (Evidence 2 and 3). These together support the claim that the added instruction was associated with measurable regressions. 
- Optionally illustrate the nature of the drift with one concrete example from the candidate artifacts (Evidence 4)-for instance, the candidate outputs that reframed the post as an orchestration/architecture discussion or included implied launch/validation text-to make the abstract description of "drift" tangible. 
- Preserve the documented relationship that the candidate was evaluated against hard gates (e.g., factual/editorial restraint and theme hierarchy) and failed them, which was the explicit reason for rejecting the candidate (Evidence 3 and 5). Do not infer motives or broader product consequences beyond the recorded decision and measurements.

### What should be de-emphasized

- De-emphasize broader product or implementation details that are not required to tell this experiment story (for example: full build/test logs, the broader Compose workflow, naming history, and unrelated prompt files) since the core claim rests on a single prompt change and its measured outcomes (Research Facts 41-43, 51-53). 
- De-emphasize attempts to explain why the drift occurred (e.g., which clause caused it or whether baseline Synthesis tension contributed) unless additional evidence is available; those are unknowns (see below). 

### Gaps and uncertainty

- Causality is not established: Research does not prove the added preservation section was the cause of the drift-only that the change coincided with consistent regressions (Unknowns: whether the added section caused the drift; Research Fact 32 framed this as insufficient to protect editorial intent). 
- Small sample and tooling limits: only three runs per condition were used and no temperature/seed overrides were available, so statistical generalization is limited (Research Facts 48, 47). 
- Model alias and provider variability: the model alias (gpt-5-mini) may map to different backends over time; Research cannot exclude provider-side variation as a contributor (Research Fact 11; Unknown: whether alias mapped to a different backend). 
- Underspecified wording: Research does not identify which exact phrase(s) in the added section (if any) produced the observed changes (Unknowns). 
- Generalizability: Research does not establish whether a revised or more specific preservation instruction, different content formats, or other model families would avoid these regressions (Unknowns).

Narrative should present this as a narrowly scoped experiment: a controlled prompt-change test that produced a reproducible negative outcome and explicit rollback, while clearly signaling the limits on causal claims and generalization.

Narrative
---------

Selected Story
A controlled prompt-change experiment added a single generic section titled "Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis" to the Narrative user prompt; this change coincided with a reproducible pattern of editorial drift-company identity and audience scope were deprioritized, orchestration was elevated to central positioning, and unsupported audience/validation claims appeared-which corresponded with measurable declines in quality scores and prompted evaluators to decline promotion and restore the baseline prompt. The main point: in this test, a small, generic preservation instruction was associated with concrete, reproducible regressions and an explicit rollback, though causality beyond the experiment is not established.

Story Progression
- Context: A single, controlled modification was made to the Narrative user prompt (only src/Nueyon.Compose.Application/Prompts/Narrative/Narrative.user.prompt.txt was changed) to add a generic "Preserve Editorial Intent from the Synthesis" section intended to protect top-level messaging.
- Development: The candidate condition produced lower mean scores (Narrative: baseline 6.00candidate 4.00; Compose: baseline 5.67candidate 3.33), three qualitative failures that recurred across runs (subordinated company identity/audience scope, elevated orchestration as central positioning, and introduced unsupported validation/audience framing), and Compose artifacts that concretely illustrated the drift (e.g., reframing content as an orchestration/architecture discussion and including implied launch/validation language). These failures caused the candidate to fail hard gates tied to theme hierarchy and factual/editorial restraint.
- Endpoint: The documented outcome is that evaluators declined to promote the candidate prompt and the production Narrative prompt was restored byte-for-byte to the baseline SHA-256 value; the experiment therefore records a reproducible negative outcome associated with that single prompt addition within the experiment's operational limits.

Critical Constraints
- Do not claim the added section definitively caused the drift; the experiment shows a coincident, reproducible association only.
- Do not generalize this result beyond the conditions tested (three runs per condition; no temperature/seed control; potential model-alias variability).
- Do not attribute the decision or motivations to an unspecified team; report only the documented action that evaluators declined promotion and restored the baseline.
- Do not claim that a different wording or a targeted preservation instruction would avoid the regressions unless supported by additional evidence.
- Do not assert benefits, performance improvements, or broader product impacts that are not documented by the experiment.

Compose
-------

A single, small prompt change produced a clear, reproducible signal: adding a generic section intended to preserve editorial intent coincided with measurable editorial drift and lower quality scores, and evaluators rolled the change back.

Context: one controlled modification was made to the Narrative user prompt file (src/Nueyon.Compose.Application/Prompts/Narrative/Narrative.user.prompt.txt) to add a short preservation instruction. No other prompt text was altered.

What happened next: the candidate prompt produced lower mean quality scores (Narrative: baseline 6.00  candidate 4.00; Compose: baseline 5.67  candidate 3.33) and repeated qualitative failures across runs. Three failure modes recurred: company identity and audience scope were de-emphasized; the content shifted to present orchestration as the central focus; and the output introduced unsupported audience- or validation-style framing.

Concrete artifacts illustrated the drift - for example, content was reframed as an orchestration/architecture discussion and included language that implied launch or validation steps that were not supported by the Narrative.

Outcome: evaluators declined to promote the candidate prompt and restored the production Narrative prompt byte-for-byte to the original baseline SHA-256 value.

Limits: this experiment shows a reproducible association between that single prompt addition and the observed regressions within the test conditions. It does not establish definitive causation or broader generality - the runs were limited (three per condition), there was no temperature/seed control, and model-alias variability remains a possibility.

Summary: within the experiment's operational limits, a small, generic preservation instruction coincided with concrete, repeatable regressions and an explicit rollback to the baseline prompt.

